  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 219 PM EDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nThe atmosphere remains quite moist and unstable, with scattered \nconvection again occurring around the area. Given the lack of \nshear, the primary threat from the storms continues to be gusty \nwinds with some marginal severe potential from wet microbursts \nmainly over our northern and eastern areas as highlighted in the \nSPC marginal risk. The other main concern continues to be flooding\ngiven the abundant moisture, slow movement of the thunderstorms, \nand very wet soils over much of the northeastern half of our area.\nAny prolonged or repeated heavy rains may result in localized \nflash flooding this afternoon into this evening. \n\nThe upper jet retreats further north and heights rise over our area \nSaturday as the upper ridge to our west expands over our region. Our \nsouthwestern counties may stay mainly dry Saturday as the cap is\nlikely to strengthen enough to hold closer to the core of the \nridge, although this is still not certain. Further north and east \nthere continues to be a chance for showers and storms with threats\nof wind and flooding similar to today. Sunday currently looks to \nhave less coverage of showers/storms, with the best chance in the \nmountains and our northeast counties. \n\nThe other concern heading into the weekend will be the heat.  High \ntemperatures in the mid 90s will be common across portions of the \ncentral and southern valley, and some spots will climb into the \nupper 90s. Heat index values will likely approach or exceed 105 in \nsome locations especially across the southern valley. A heat \nadvisory issued earlier today will remain in effect through Sunday \nfor the southern valley and southern Plateau. The heat advisory may \nend up needing to be extended further north into the central valley \nby later shifts. Monday also looks quite hot but with more \nuncertainty, as short wave energy moving by to our north suppresses \nthe ridge and pushes a weak summertime cold front toward our area \nincreasing the chances for showers and storms. Given uncertainty on \ntiming of precipitation and cloud cover that may impact temperatures \nthat far out, will keep Monday out of the heat advisory for now. \n\nFurther out, models generally show some drying for the mid week \nperiod, with the NBM showing a mostly dry day Wednesday before \nchances for showers and storms increases again Thursday and Friday. \nIt will continue to be hot for the Tuesday through Friday time \nframe, but currently the heat looks likely to be less oppressive \nthan during the weekend.",
